## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical risk management when relying on external data and requires a concrete rollout plan across regions with resilience patterns and measurable outcomes.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags and canary deployments
- SLOs and data freshness metrics
- Resilience patterns: circuit breakers, fallback caches
- Rollout artifacts, runbooks, and incident drills

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lightweight circuit-breaker with fallback
class Breaker {
  constructor(threshold=3) { this.failures=0; this.open=false; }
  async call(fn) {
    if (this.open) throw new Error('Circuit open');
    try { return await fn(); } catch (e) { this.failures++; if (this.failures >= threshold) this.open=true; throw e; }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate ETA accuracy during canary without impacting users?
- How would you design post-incident reviews and remedial actions?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests balancing reliability and speed in a multi-team setting with a live data pipeline. It pushes candidates to articulate ownership, metrics, and governance for a delicate system upgrade.

## Key Concepts

- Event-driven architecture, exactly-once delivery, idempotency
- Cross-team ownership & governance, capacity planning
- Dual-track planning, go/no-go gates, risk mitigation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example idempotent handler
function handleEvent(event) {
  const id = event.id;
  if (hasProcessed(id)) return;
  markProcessed(id);
  process(event);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure success and adapt if MTTR targets aren’t met?
- What would trigger a rollback vs. feature flag deprecation in production?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to translate reliability into a product mindset across multiple teams, define measurable outcomes, and design practical experiments that balance speed and quality.

## Key Concepts

- Observability as a product
- SLOs and error budgets
- Instrumentation with OpenTelemetry
- Incident rituals and blameless postmortems
- Toil reduction experiments

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal OpenTelemetry trace init (illustrative)
import { trace } from '@opentelemetry/api';
const t = trace.getTracer('service');
const span = t.startSpan('request');
// business logic
span.end();
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this program across eight squads? 
- Which metrics would you track beyond MTTR and velocity?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests ability to design governance for cross-team experimentation, balancing speed with reliability and data integrity.

## Key Concepts
- KPI ownership and charter
- Preflight power analysis and data-quality gates
- Guardrails: latency, error rate, quotas
- Feature flags, canary and cohort rollouts
- Artifacts: experiment charter, data map, rollback plan

## Code Example
```javascript
function shouldRollout(experiment, metrics) {
  const {power, mde, dataQuality} = metrics;
  if (!experiment.flagsEnabled) return false;
  if (power < experiment.requiredPower) return false;
  if (!dataQuality.ok) return false;
  if (metrics.latency > experiment.maxLatency) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would thresholds vary by product area?
- How would you measure guardrail impact on velocity and reliability?
